To establish a connection between a Python script that writes data and a JavaScript script that listens for the data, you can use several methods depending on your specific requirements and environment setup. Below is an example of how this could be done using sockets in Python.

### Python Script (writing side)

```python
import socket

# Create a TCP/IP socket
sock = socket.socket(socket.AF_INET, socket.SOCK_STREAM)

# Define the server's IP address and port number
server_address = ('localhost', 10000)
print(f"Connecting to {server_address}")

# Connect the socket to the server
sock.connect(server_address)

try:
    # Write some data to the socket
    message = "Hello from Python"
    sock.sendall(message.encode('utf-8'))

finally:
    print("Closing socket")
    sock.close()
```

### JavaScript Script (listening side)

In the following example, we will use Node.js with `http` module to listen for incoming connections.

First, ensure you have Node.js installed. Then create two files: `index.html` and `server.js`.

#### index.html

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>Socket Test</title>
</head>
<body>
    <script>
        const PORT = 10000;

        // Listen for incoming requests
        const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {
            if (req.method === 'GET' && req.url === '/') {
                res.writeHead(200, {'Content-Type': 'text/plain'});
                res.end(`Welcome to Socket.io!`);
            } else {
                res.writeHead(404);
                res.end();
            }
        });

        // Start the server on the specified port
        server.listen(PORT, () => console.log(`Server running at http://localhost:${PORT}`));
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

